WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.139 --> 00:00:03.339
With all the under the hood stuff out of the way,

2
00:00:03.339 --> 00:00:06.110
let's talk about scripting and blueprint.

3
00:00:06.110 --> 00:00:08.310
Blueprint is an extensive suite of tools

4
00:00:08.310 --> 00:00:11.029
built on top of a C++ foundation,

5
00:00:11.029 --> 00:00:13.869
allowing developers to start creating immediately.

6
00:00:13.869 --> 00:00:15.550
It's built directly into the editor

7
00:00:15.550 --> 00:00:17.149
and pairing any member of the team,

8
00:00:17.149 --> 00:00:20.030
be they design, art, or anything in between,

9
00:00:20.030 --> 00:00:21.469
to start developing their ideas

10
00:00:21.469 --> 00:00:23.789
with a significantly reduced iteration time,

11
00:00:23.789 --> 00:00:25.870
making it ideal for testing out ideas,

12
00:00:25.870 --> 00:00:27.910
making it great for prototyping.

13
00:00:27.949 --> 00:00:31.850
But, well-structured blueprint can and should

14
00:00:31.850 --> 00:00:33.450
be considered production code,

15
00:00:33.450 --> 00:00:35.689
and mechanics built in blueprint shouldn't be dismissed

16
00:00:35.689 --> 00:00:38.539
because they're not in native C++.

17
00:00:38.539 --> 00:00:40.500
Traditional concepts that apply to programming

18
00:00:40.500 --> 00:00:42.280
also apply here, as blueprint is,

19
00:00:42.280 --> 00:00:43.820
for all intents and purposes,

20
00:00:43.820 --> 00:00:45.820
a fully featured, fully functional,

21
00:00:45.820 --> 00:00:47.659
and feature-rich programming environment

22
00:00:47.659 --> 00:00:49.750
and should be treated as such.

23
00:00:49.750 --> 00:00:50.969
When developing in blueprint,

24
00:00:50.969 --> 00:00:52.270
it's important to consider factors

25
00:00:52.270 --> 00:00:54.509
that you would otherwise consider in regular code.

26
00:00:54.509 --> 00:00:56.149
Is this going to kill my frame rate?

27
00:00:56.149 --> 00:00:58.350
Is this math function as efficient as it could be?

28
00:00:59.189 --> 00:01:01.820
Have I built my blueprint in an object-orientated manner?

29
00:01:01.820 --> 00:01:02.640
With that in mind,

30
00:01:02.640 --> 00:01:04.140
where does that leave the traditional engineer

31
00:01:04.140 --> 00:01:07.019
when it comes to such a powerful scripting engine?

32
00:01:07.019 --> 00:01:08.420
There's a whole spectrum of tools

33
00:01:08.420 --> 00:01:09.540
that we can develop as engineers

34
00:01:09.540 --> 00:01:11.140
to give our designers and artists,

35
00:01:11.140 --> 00:01:12.180
as well as ourselves,

36
00:01:12.180 --> 00:01:15.579
more fleshed out tools when working in the raw C++.

37
00:01:15.579 --> 00:01:18.599
Ultimately, blueprint sits on top of the project's code,

38
00:01:18.599 --> 00:01:20.260
so we can expose our areas of the code

39
00:01:20.260 --> 00:01:22.939
to give the team more gadgets to play with.

40
00:01:22.939 --> 00:01:25.019
Exposing at functionality is staggeringly easy

41
00:01:25.019 --> 00:01:26.939
when using Unreal's reflection system,

42
00:01:26.939 --> 00:01:29.819
specifically the U function and U property specifiers

43
00:01:29.819 --> 00:01:32.109
and their associated meta.

44
00:01:32.109 --> 00:01:34.629
As an expansion of the U function specifiers,

45
00:01:34.629 --> 00:01:36.590
we've got the blueprint callable.

46
00:01:36.590 --> 00:01:37.950
So imagine we've got a complex set

47
00:01:37.950 --> 00:01:39.870
of mathematical functions that really do benefit

48
00:01:39.870 --> 00:01:41.959
from being in C++.

49
00:01:41.959 --> 00:01:44.180
We can define the complex function in code

50
00:01:44.180 --> 00:01:46.840
and have our blueprint layer call it.

51
00:01:46.840 --> 00:01:48.939
The blueprint callable with a const keyword

52
00:01:48.939 --> 00:01:50.219
or blueprint pure

53
00:01:50.219 --> 00:01:53.040
gives us a single getter node in blueprint.

54
00:01:53.040 --> 00:01:55.760
Typically, most blueprint nodes need to be executed,

55
00:01:55.760 --> 00:01:57.019
marking a function as const

56
00:01:57.019 --> 00:01:59.879
or indeed having it marked as a blueprint pure meta,

57
00:01:59.879 --> 00:02:01.900
will give us a node that simply pulls the value

58
00:02:01.900 --> 00:02:03.939
without executing anything at all.

59
00:02:03.939 --> 00:02:06.019
Blueprint implementable event is a function

60
00:02:06.019 --> 00:02:07.239
that we can call from code,

61
00:02:07.239 --> 00:02:09.659
but execute it on the blueprint itself.

62
00:02:09.659 --> 00:02:11.520
So say we've got some cosmetic functionality

63
00:02:11.520 --> 00:02:13.020
that we want to trigger from code,

64
00:02:13.020 --> 00:02:14.939
but we don't have access to the required assets

65
00:02:14.939 --> 00:02:17.620
or components to manage that effect in the code.

66
00:02:17.620 --> 00:02:18.460
It's not a problem,

67
00:02:18.460 --> 00:02:20.340
we can create a blueprint implementable event

68
00:02:20.340 --> 00:02:22.900
and hook up our cosmetics on the blueprint graph.

69
00:02:22.900 --> 00:02:24.740
A blueprint native event is very similar

70
00:02:24.740 --> 00:02:26.659
to a blueprint implementable event,

71
00:02:27.180 --> 00:02:29.180
but it also has a native implementation.

72
00:02:29.180 --> 00:02:31.939
If this function isn't overridden on the blueprint graph,

73
00:02:31.939 --> 00:02:35.229
the native implementation will be called instead.

74
00:02:35.229 --> 00:02:39.120
Moving on to you property, we have UI min and UI max.

75
00:02:39.120 --> 00:02:41.099
This provides a slider in editor for the user

76
00:02:41.099 --> 00:02:44.300
to enter a float value clamped at the entered values.

77
00:02:44.300 --> 00:02:46.180
It does not prevent the user from entering a value

78
00:02:46.180 --> 00:02:48.460
outside of the desired bounds however.

79
00:02:48.460 --> 00:02:51.500
For that, we need to look to clamp min and clamp max,

80
00:02:51.500 --> 00:02:53.860
which will cause the values entered into a float parameter

81
00:02:53.860 --> 00:02:56.099
to be bound with this range.

82
00:02:56.099 --> 00:02:57.939
From there, we can look at display name.

83
00:02:57.939 --> 00:02:59.300
This changes what name is displayed

84
00:02:59.300 --> 00:03:01.300
besides this parameter in the editor.

85
00:03:01.300 --> 00:03:02.439
And it's useful for when we want

86
00:03:02.439 --> 00:03:03.860
a very descriptive naming code,

87
00:03:03.860 --> 00:03:07.599
but maybe a more succinct contextual one in the editor.

88
00:03:07.599 --> 00:03:10.810
From there, we have blueprint getter and blueprint setter.

89
00:03:10.810 --> 00:03:13.210
This allows us to manually define a function to call

90
00:03:13.210 --> 00:03:16.370
when directly getting and setting a member variable.

91
00:03:16.370 --> 00:03:18.409
It's useful if we want to call another piece of code

92
00:03:18.409 --> 00:03:22.750
directly whenever a value changes in blueprint.

93
00:03:22.750 --> 00:03:25.710
As mentioned, blueprint is a fully featured language

94
00:03:25.710 --> 00:03:28.370
and as such supports interfaces.

95
00:03:28.370 --> 00:03:30.530
Interfaces can be defined in C++

96
00:03:30.530 --> 00:03:32.050
and implemented in blueprint

97
00:03:32.050 --> 00:03:33.590
with any of its virtual functions

98
00:03:33.590 --> 00:03:37.139
free to implement the U function markup.

99
00:03:37.139 --> 00:03:39.000
In order to achieve this expansion though,

100
00:03:39.000 --> 00:03:40.340
we must mark up our interface

101
00:03:40.340 --> 00:03:44.229
with the U interface blueprintable specifier.

102
00:03:44.229 --> 00:03:45.629
When calling an interface function

103
00:03:45.629 --> 00:03:47.449
on a blueprint class from native,

104
00:03:47.449 --> 00:03:49.469
we first have to check the object that we're working with

105
00:03:49.469 --> 00:03:52.750
actually implements this interface at all.

106
00:03:52.750 --> 00:03:54.590
Alternatively, you could cast the object

107
00:03:54.590 --> 00:03:56.349
to the intended interface.

108
00:03:56.389 --> 00:03:59.069
That too would let us know if it implements it.

109
00:03:59.069 --> 00:04:01.349
If it does, we can go ahead and call the function

110
00:04:01.349 --> 00:04:03.889
using a statically generated execute function

111
00:04:03.889 --> 00:04:06.080
following the above syntax.

112
00:04:06.080 --> 00:04:07.639
The parameters being the object

113
00:04:07.639 --> 00:04:09.280
we wish to call the function on,

114
00:04:09.280 --> 00:04:11.020
followed by any parameters that are required

115
00:04:11.020 --> 00:04:13.120
for that function signature.

116
00:04:13.120 --> 00:04:15.240
Unreal's architecture has an incredible degree

117
00:04:15.240 --> 00:04:16.819
of flexibility and helps put power

118
00:04:16.819 --> 00:04:18.759
into designers and artists hands

119
00:04:18.759 --> 00:04:20.959
when getting their ideas out and working in the project

120
00:04:20.959 --> 00:04:23.139
and to a production level.

121
00:04:23.139 --> 00:04:25.319
The idea that blueprint is for non-programmers

122
00:04:25.319 --> 00:04:28.079
or for prototyping is demonstrably incorrect

123
00:04:28.079 --> 00:04:29.720
and the two methods of development,

124
00:04:29.720 --> 00:04:32.240
that being traditional engineering and blueprint

125
00:04:32.240 --> 00:04:34.160
should be seen as working hand in hand,

126
00:04:34.160 --> 00:04:35.800
not in opposition to each other.
